From ud35e483a32894a6c at groups.google.com  Wed May  3 03:27:49 2017
From: ud35e483a32894a6c at groups.google.com (Chenhui Liu)
Date: Wed, 03 May 2017 03:27:49 +0000
Subject: RW(1) model
Message-ID: <_0jgIi5R6vI.6UUjtYdGAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/_0jgIi5R6vI/m/6UUjtYdGAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi guys,

I have a question about the structure of the RW(1) model in INLA.

The code of rw(1) in INLA is:
f(id, model="rw1")

On the website of INLA, it says the RW(1) is:
x[i] = x[i-1] + e, e~N(0, sigma2)

While in the paper of "Spatial and patio-temporal models with R-INLA", on page 7, it says the

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-4GjSCmxKcUE/WQlNsR0fQ6I/AAAAAAAAAMk/Ihojg1h3Be8Mn3QwUgqPljVK0HnZiI70ACLcB/s1600/Untitled.jpg>

with seemly the same rw(1) code.

Does anyone know what is the difference of these two cases? When we write the f(id, model ="rw1"), which structure is used?

Thanks
Blangiardo, Marta, et al. "Spatial and spatio-temporal models with R-INLA." Spatial and spatio-temporal epidemiology 7 (2013): 39-55.

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Wed May  3 05:35:24 2017
